<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Desmond Tutu</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1931">1931</a>–<a href="/years/2021">2021</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Archbishop Desmond Tutu</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Desmond Tutu was a South African Anglican cleric and anti-apartheid campaigner, Archbishop of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-town">Cape Town</a> from 1986 to 1996. He won the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nobel-peace-prize">Nobel Peace Prize</a> in 1984 for his non-violent opposition to apartheid. After 1994 he chaired the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/truth-and-reconciliation-commission-south-africa">Truth and Reconciliation Commission</a>, which heard testimony from victims and perpetrators of apartheid-era abuses, and he popularised the phrase &quot;Rainbow Nation&quot;.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/desmond-tutu.jpg" alt="Desmond Tutu" width="320" height="480" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Benny Gool · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Archbishop-Tutu-medium.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Truth and Reconciliation Commission, chaired by <b>Archbishop Desmond Tutu</b> from 1996, became a model studied by many societies emerging from conflict.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-south-africa">Republic of South Africa · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The churches gave the movement shelter and a voice: <b>Desmond Tutu</b>, general secretary of the South African Council of Churches and from 1986 Archbishop of Cape Town, received the Nobel Peace Prize in 1984, and the Afrikaner minister Beyers Naudé left the ministry of his church to found…</blockquote><a href="/movement/anti-apartheid-movement">Anti-apartheid movement · People</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Munsieville, in what is now <a href="/countries/south-africa">South Africa</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-town">Cape Town</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/south-africa">South Africa</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=-26.08,27.75&amp;year=1989" aria-label="Munsieville on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5283 3122 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Desmond Tutu"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5733 3392h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5733 3392h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=-26.08,27.75&amp;year=1989">What was here in 1989? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
